@page "/documentation"
<PageTitle>Documentation</PageTitle>
<div class="container">
    <!--Title-->
    <div>
        <h1 class="fw-bold">TruSql</h1>
        <div class="d-flex gap-2 mb-2">
            <span class="badge bg-primary">C#</span>
            <span class="badge bg-success">Custom AST Compiler</span>
            <span class="badge bg-info text-dark">Dapper</span>
        </div>
    </div>
    <hr />
    <!--Introduction-->
    <div class="mb-5">
        <h2 class="fw-bold">Introduction</h2>
        <h4>SQL Query Builder is a tool for building SQL queries dynamically while keeping them safe and validated.</h4>
        <h5 class="mt-3 fw-bold"></h5>
        <div class="card">
            <div class="card-header bg-info-subtle">
                A C# SQL query builder featuring:
            </div>
            <div class="card-body">
                <ul class="my-2">
                    <li>Dynamic query construction</li>
                    <li>Custom SQL tokenizer, parser, and AST</li>
                    <li>Type-aware expression analysis</li>
                    <li>Query validation</li>
                    <li>Parameterized SQL generation</li>
                    <li>Thread-safe query building</li>
                </ul>
            </div>
        </div>
    </div>
    <!--Quick Start-->
    <div class="mb-5">
        <h2 class="fw-bold">Quick Start</h2>
        <p>Creating a SQL query with SQL Query Builder is straightforward. You can also try the examples interactively in the <NavLink href="playground#main-playground">Playground</NavLink></p>
        <div class="p-3">
            <ol>
                <li>
                    <p>
                        Create a new query builder using <code>@("QueryBuilder.Create<T>()")</code>. The generic type T represents the target DTO that the generated query will map to. In this example, RamReadModel is used as the target DTO.
                    </p>
                    <pre class="bg-dark rounded rounded-3 p-3 text-info-emphasis">
                    @("QueryBuilder.Create<RamReadModel>();")
                </pre>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <p>
                        Add a data source with <code>@("FROM<T>(string alias)")</code>. This tells SQL Query Builder which table/model is being queried and allows it to validate referenced properties against the model.
                    </p>
                    <pre class="bg-dark rounded rounded-3 p-3 text-info-emphasis">
                    @("QueryBuilder\n\t.Create<RamReadModel>()")@("\n\t.From<RamBaseModel>(\"r\");")
                </pre>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <p>
                        After providing data source using <code>FROM&lt;T&gt;</code> now you can select the column you want using <code>Select(string columns)</code> API here i'm selecting name and price from ram base model then the code check if it exist in DTO target in this case it's ram read model
                    </p>
                    <pre class="bg-dark rounded rounded-3 p-3 text-info-emphasis">
                    @("QueryBuilder\n\t.Create<RamReadModel>()")@("\n\t.From<RamBaseModel>(\"r\")")@("\n\t.Select(\"r.name, r.price\");")
                </pre>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <p>
                        After selecting columns, you can then add joins or filters, validate the base query to create a cache, or build the query straight away.
                    </p>

                    <p>
                        Here, let's add a join using the API
                        <code>Join&lt;T&gt;(string alias, string joinCondition, SQLJoinType joinType)</code>.
                        The alias and join condition are mandatory, while the join type is optional and defaults to <code>INNER</code>.
                    </p>

                    <pre class="bg-dark rounded rounded-3 p-3 text-info-emphasis">
                    @("QueryBuilder\n\t.Create<RamReadModel>()")@("\n\t.From<RamBaseModel>(\"r\")")@("\n\t.Select(\"r.name, r.price\")")@("\n\t.Join<BrandBaseModel>(\"b\", \"b.id = r.Brandid\");")
                </pre>

                    <div class="p-3 bg-info-subtle border-start border-4 border-info rounded-end shadow-sm mb-4">
                        <div class="d-flex align-items-center mb-1">
                            <i class="bi bi-lightbulb-fill text-success me-2 fs-5"></i>
                            <span class="fw-bold text-uppercase text-success fs-7 tracking-wide">Implementation Note</span>
                        </div>

                        <p class="mb-0 text-secondary">
                            Although <code>Join</code> appears after <code>Select</code> in this example,
                            the query builder processes the data sources before validating the selected columns.
                            This allows properties introduced by joins to be available when <code>Select</code> is validated.
                        </p>
                    </div>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <p>
                        In this example, we will see how to create a query cache so you can reuse a pre-built base query without having to rebuild the base components every time. This is done using the <code>ValidateBaseQuery()</code> API.
                    </p>
                    <p>
                        If the same base query will be reused with different filters, call <code>CopyBase()</code> to create a lightweight copy.
                        The copied base includes the <code>FROM</code>, <code>SELECT</code>, and <code>JOIN</code> clauses. Clauses like <code>WHERE</code>, <code>HAVING</code>, <code>GROUP BY</code>, <code>ORDER BY</code>, and <code>LIMIT</code> are excluded.
                    </p>

                    <!-- Non-dismissible Warning Alert -->
                    <div class="alert alert-warning" role="alert">
                        <h5 class="alert-heading d-flex align-items-center gap-2 fw-bold mb-2">
                            <i class="bi bi-exclamation-triangle-fill"></i> WARNING
                        </h5>
                        <p class="mb-0">
                            If you attempt to copy a query without calling <code>ValidateBaseQuery()</code> first, the query builder will still work correctly; however, it will construct the entire query AST from scratch rather than leveraging the cached base.
                        </p>
                    </div>

                    <h6 class="fw-bold mt-3">1. Validating and Caching the Base Query</h6>
                    <pre class="bg-dark text-info-emphasis rounded-3 p-3">
                        <code>var cache = QueryBuilder
                .Create&lt;RamReadModel&gt;()
                .From&lt;RamBaseModel&gt;("r")
                .Select("r.name, r.price")
                .Join&lt;BrandBaseModel&gt;("b", "b.id = r.Brandid")
                .ValidateBaseQuery();
                        </code>
                    </pre>

                    <h6 class="fw-bold mt-3">2. Copying the Cached Query Base</h6>
                    <pre class="bg-dark text-info-emphasis rounded-3 p-3"><code>var copy = cache.CopyBase();</code></pre>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <p>
                        You can customize the query using clauses such as <code>Having(string input)</code>, <code>Where(string input)</code>, <code>GroupBy(string input)</code>, <code>OrderBy(string input)</code>, <code>Limit(int input)</code> lets do example using all of them
                    </p>
                    <pre class="bg-dark rounded rounded-3 p-3 text-info-emphasis">
                    @("QueryBuilder\n\t.Create<RamReadModel>()")@("\n\t.From<RamBaseModel>(\"r\")")@("\n\t.Select(\"r.name, r.price\")")@("\n\t.Join<BrandBaseModel>(\"b\", \"b.id = r.Brandid\")")@("\n\t.Where(\"r.name = 'corsair'\")")@("\n\t.GroupBy(\"r.name\")")@("\n\t.Having(\"r.price < 100\")")@("\n\t.OrderBy(\"r.price\")")@("\n\t.Limit(15);")
                </pre>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <p>
                        Finally you can build the query and the parameters using <code>.Build()</code> you can build the query at anytime after <code>.Select()</code>
                    </p>
                    <pre class="bg-dark rounded rounded-3 p-3 text-info-emphasis">
                    @("QueryBuilder\n\t.Create<RamReadModel>()")@("\n\t.From<RamBaseModel>(\"r\")")@("\n\t.Select(\"r.name, r.price\")")@("\n\t.Join<BrandBaseModel>(\"b\", \"b.id = r.Brandid\")")@("\n\t.Where(\"r.name = 'corsair'\")")@("\n\t.GroupBy(\"r.name\")")@("\n\t.Having(\"r.price < 100\")")@("\n\t.OrderBy(\"r.price\")")@("\n\t.Limit(15)\n\t.Build();")
                </pre>
                </li>
            </ol>
        </div>
