    <!--Data Flow-->
    <div class="mb-5">
        <h2 class="fw-bold">API Data Flow</h2>
        <p>In here we will discuss how input gets turned into an SQL query step by step in this example will use <code>r.Price > 100</code> as an example!</p>
        <div class="p-3">
            <ol class="d-inline-grid gap-2">
                <li>
                    First the program take the input from user in this case we use <code>r.Price > 100</code> and put it into the tokenizer as a parameter.
                </li>
                <li>
                    Then the Tokenizer get the input and turn it into a SQL Tokenizer Context and begin tokenizing the input in this case <code>r.Price > 100</code> turn into:
                    <table class="table table-hover table-striped my-3">
                        <thead class="fw-bold">
                            <tr>
                                <th>#</th>
                                <th>Value</th>
                                <th>Token</th>
                            </tr>
                        </thead>
                        <tbody>
                            <tr>
                                <th>1</th>
                                <th>r</th>
                                <th>Identifier</th>
                            </tr>
                            <tr>
                                <th>2</th>
                                <th>.</th>
                                <th>Dot</th>
                            </tr>
                            <tr>
                                <th>3</th>
                                <th>Price</th>
                                <th>Identifier</th>
                            </tr>
                            <tr>
                                <th>4</th>
                                <th>></th>
                                <th>GreaterThan</th>
                            </tr>
                            <tr>
                                <th>5</th>
                                <th>100</th>
                                <th>IntegerLiteral</th>
                            </tr>
                        </tbody>

                    </table>
                </li>
                <li>
                    After that tokenizer then output it into SQL Parser Context which contain the tokens from before and then the parser take the context to parse it into an AST, here the input <code>r.Price > 100</code> turn into a Binary Expression like this:
                    <ul class="list-unstyled ps-3 mt-2 mb-0">
                        <li><strong>Binary Expression</strong></li>
                        <li>Left = r.Price (Column Expression)</li>
                        <li>Operator = GreaterThan</li>
                        <li>Right = 100 (Literal Expression)</li>
                    </ul>
                </li>
                <li>
                    After the input turn into an AST then the program take that ast and put it into a Semantic Analyzer where it analyze the input if it valid first the analyzer analyze the AST and turn it into a metadata like this:
                    <ol class="ps-3 m-3 mb-0">
                        <li>Left is r.Price for this example lets say price is INT then it will return as metadata int</li>
                        <li>Right is 100 here its a literal integer so it return as metadata int too</li>
                        <li>After that it take the operator in this case its a comparison operator so both side need to be a numerical type for it not to get rejected</li>
                    </ol>
                </li>
                <li>
                    After the validation and the Validation doesn't throw any error then the program continue to the next step which is creating the query, here the SQL generator take the AST and then create a string query and parameterize the value at the same time 
                </li>
                <li>Finally, the program calls <code>.Build()</code> to materialize the SQL query and its associated parameters, returning the completed result.</li>
            </ol>
        </div>
