    <!--Tokenizer-->
    <div class="mb-5">
        <h2 class="fw-bold">Tokenizer (Lexical Analysis)</h2>
        <p>The tokenizer operates as a character-by-character scanner. Using state-tracking and lookahead logic, it loops through the string and decides how to group characters based on what type of token they belong to.</p>
        <p>
            For example, watching how the lexer processes <code>r.Price > 100</code> character-by-character reveals its internal decision-making:
        </p>

        <div class="p-3">
            <ul class="list-unstyled ps-3 mt-2 mb-0">
                <li class="mb-3">
                    <strong>1. Spotting the first letter (<code>r</code>):</strong>
                    The scanner reads the first character. Because it's an alphabetical letter, it triggers the <code>ParseIdentifier()</code> state, continuing to consume characters until it hits a non-identifier character (the dot). It emits <code>Identifier("r")</code>.
                </li>
                <li class="mb-3">
                    <strong>2. Encountering the dot (<code>.</code>):</strong>
                    The scanner hits a punctuation mark. It doesn't need to loop or look ahead; it immediately recognizes it as a member-access separator and emits <code>Dot</code>.
                </li>
                <li class="mb-3">
                    <strong>3. Parsing the property name (<code>Price</code>):</strong>
                    Just like step 1, hitting the letter <code>P</code> triggers the identifier-parsing loop again, consuming <code>r-i-c-e</code> and emitting <code>Identifier("Price")</code>.
                </li>
                <li class="mb-3">
                    <strong>4. Evaluating the operator (<code>></code>):</strong>
                    The scanner encounters a symbol. It checks if a compound operator could follow (like <code>>=</code>), but since the next character is a space, it finalizes and emits <code>GreaterThan</code>.
                </li>
                <li class="mb-3">
                    <strong>5. Consuming the literal (<code>100</code>):</strong>
                    When the scanner hits a digit (<code>1</code>), it shifts into a numeric parsing state (<code>ParseNumber()</code>), consuming the whole number sequence and emitting <code>IntegerLiteral("100")</code>.
                </li>
            </ul>
        </div>

        <p class="mt-3">
            To test these lexer states and see how edge cases (like strings or whitespace) are handled live, try the <NavLink href="tokenizer">Tokenizer Visualizer</NavLink>.
        </p>
