    <!--Parser-->
    <div class="mb-5">
        <h2 class="fw-bold">Parser</h2>
        <p>The parser operates on tokens produced by the tokenizer it works by analyzing each token and deciding which expression matches the current tokens </p>
        <p>
            For example, let's use <code>r.Price > 100</code> input that is already being tokenized by the tokenizer and see what happens
        </p>
        <div class="p-3">
            <ol class="d-inline-grid gap-3">
                <li>
                    Parse the left-hand expression. The parser starts at <code>ParseOR()</code> and descends through the parsing methods until it reaches <code>ParsePrimary()</code>. The identifier r is then handled by <code>ParseIdentifierOrFunction()</code>.
                </li>
                <li>
                    Resolve the column reference. The parser checks whether the identifier is followed by an opening parenthesis to determine whether it represents a function call. Since it does not, the parser continues checking for a dotted identifier or wildcard reference. It consumes the dot (.) and the following identifier (Price), constructing the column reference <code>r.Price</code>.
                </li>
                <li>
                    Parse the comparison operator. Once the left-hand expression has been parsed, <code>ParsePredicate()</code> recognizes the greater-than operator (>). The parser validates the operator against the supported comparison operators and consumes it.
                </li>
                <li>
                    Parse the right-hand expression. The parser processes the integer literal 100 through <code>ParseLiteral()</code>, producing a <code>Literal Expression</code> node.
                </li>
                <li>
                    Construct the final expression. With both operands and the comparison operator available, the parser constructs a <code>Binary Expression</code> node. Since the next token is EOF, parsing completes successfully, returning the resulting AST.
                </li>
            </ol>
            <p>You can test the parser yourself in <NavLink href="ast">AST Visualizer</NavLink></p>
        </div>
