    <!--Analyzer-->
    <div class="mb-5">
        <h2 class="fw-bold">Semantic Analyzer</h2>
        <p>
            The Semantic Analyzer validates the AST by determining the metadata of each expression and checking whether the operations are semantically valid.
        </p>
        <p>
            Lets use <code>r.Price > 100</code> as an example on how the analyzer handle the AST
        </p>
        <div class="p-3">
            <ol class="d-inline-grid gap-3">
                <li>
                    Analyze the expression. The analyzer receives the root Binary Expression and processes it through <code>AnalyzeExpression()</code>, which dispatches analysis based on the expression type..
                </li>
                <li>
                    Analyze the binary expression. The analyzer proceeds to <code>AnalyzeBinary()</code> and identifies the operator as a comparison. It then delegates to the comparison analysis logic.
                </li>
                <li>
                    Analyze both operands. The analyzer recursively analyzes the left and right expressions. For this example, <code>r.Price</code> resolves to an <code>integer</code> column, while <code>100</code> is an <code>integer</code> literal. Both operands therefore produce <code>int</code> metadata.
                </li>
                <li>
                    Validate the comparison. The analyzer checks whether the operand types are compatible with the greater-than operator. Since both operands are <code>numeric</code>, the comparison is valid.
                </li>
                <li>
                    Return the result metadata. The comparison produces <code>boolean</code> metadata, representing the result type of the entire expression <code>r.Price</code> > 100.
                </li>
            </ol>
        </div>
